CVE-2024-1234 targets AWS IAM policies granting `sts:AssumeRole` without proper `Condition` constraints. Example vulnerable policy:
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::*:role/*Admin*"
  }]
}
```
Attackers bypass `ArnLike` by using role ARNs encoded with URL-safe characters (e.g., `arn%3Aaws%3Aiam%3A%3A123456789012%3Arole%2FAdmin` via `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity`). Terraform automation (v1.5+) automates exploitation:
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "malicious" {
  name = "AdminRole"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { Federated = "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com" }
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
    }]
  })
}
```

Real-world abuse by Storm-0558 (Microsoft breach 2023) used similar IAM chained misconfigurations. Mitigations include SCPs (e.g., `aws:ResourceOrgID`), IAM Access Analyzer, and `sts:AssumeRole` restrictions via `aws:RequestTag/`.

